THE SHAPE -- the two codes fail differently, not by a constant factor

  diff Manchester
    low rate (<= 3/s, outages do not overlap):  p = 0.770 breaks per
    impulse, tau = 0.73 ms per break  ->  independent-outage model
    muted(r) = 1 - exp(-r / 1779), checked against the measurements:
           1/s   measured   0.057%   model   0.056%   breaks/impulse 0.758
          10/s   measured   0.577%   model   0.561%   breaks/impulse 0.797
         100/s   measured   5.142%   model   5.466%   breaks/impulse 0.691
         300/s   measured  14.343%   model  15.518%   breaks/impulse 0.603
         500/s   measured  22.389%   model  24.501%   breaks/impulse 0.532
        1000/s   measured  38.403%   model  42.999%   breaks/impulse 0.387

  AMI
    low rate (<= 3/s, outages do not overlap):  p = 0.144 breaks per
    impulse, tau = 0.73 ms per break  ->  independent-outage model
    muted(r) = 1 - exp(-r / 9467), checked against the measurements:
           1/s   measured   0.009%   model   0.011%   breaks/impulse 0.125
          10/s   measured   0.099%   model   0.106%   breaks/impulse 0.130
         100/s   measured   0.972%   model   1.051%   breaks/impulse 0.128
         300/s   measured   3.802%   model   3.119%   breaks/impulse 0.148
         500/s   measured   8.582%   model   5.144%   breaks/impulse 0.168
        1000/s   measured  99.957%   model  10.024%   breaks/impulse 0.000

  Differential Manchester follows the model to within ~10-15% across the
  whole range.  Each impulse above the comparator threshold causes a slip
  (0.77 per impulse at low rate), each slip costs ~0.76 ms, and outages
  simply add.  Breaks per impulse falls slowly at high rate (0.68 at 100/s,
  0.37 at 1000/s) because one outage starts absorbing several impulses.

  AMI does NOT follow it.  Breaks per impulse stays near 0.13 -- about a
  sixth of differential Manchester -- and muting grows gently to 9.4% at
  500/s, then the link COLLAPSES -- see the 500-1000/s rows for where.
  AMI does not slip (line-codes.md 4.1: bit count preserved at every
  impulse rate), so its breaks are accumulated bit errors, and the likely
  mechanism for the collapse is F-bit errors reaching the +2/-1 counter's
  one-in-three trip point, beyond which nothing holds -- a threshold, not
  an exponential.  Hypothesis, consistent with the shape; not measured.

  So "AMI is Nx better" has no single N: ~5-6x on breaks per second through
  the working range, falling to ~3x on muted time -- and from 900/s on, AMI is muted MORE than differential Manchester.

  wall time 1392 s
